      "lesson": {
        "title": "Writing for real digital audiences",
        "objective": "Interpret and compose short digital messages by connecting communicative purpose, audience, organization, register and contextual clues.",
        "priorKnowledge": ["Recognize common English vocabulary in context", "Identify who is speaking and what a short text is about"],
        "keyConcepts": ["Purpose and audience guide wording and organization", "Register ranges from informal to formal and depends on relationship and setting", "Links, images, emojis and layout can add meaning but must be interpreted in context"],
        "progressiveExplanation": ["Before reading a message, identify its situation: who sent it, to whom, through which channel and for what purpose. A reminder, invitation and complaint can use similar words but require different responses.", "Look at organization and register. A subject line, greeting, short paragraphs and explicit request help a formal email. A group chat may be brief and informal, but clarity and respect still matter.", "Digital texts can combine words with links, icons, images and timing. Treat those signals as evidence, not proof: a forwarded message is not verified merely because it looks official, and an emoji can soften or change a tone depending on the relationship."],
        "workedExample": "Message: “Hi Maya, could you send the final slide deck by 3 p.m.? I need to check the captions before the team meeting. Thanks!” The greeting and “could you” create a polite, moderately informal request. The deadline and reason are explicit, so Maya can act without guessing. If the same request were sent to an unfamiliar institution, a more formal greeting and subject line might be appropriate.",
        "everydayApplication": "Careful digital communication supports study groups, applications, collaborative projects and responsible evaluation of online information.",
        "commonErrors": ["Choosing a register without considering the audience", "Assuming a message is trustworthy because it has a logo or urgent wording", "Writing an unclear request without a deadline or context"],
        "summary": "Read digital messages through purpose, audience, register and evidence; make requests clear and verify claims before sharing them.",
