        "contentMarkdown": "## Objetivo\nInterpret and compose short digital messages by connecting communicative purpose, audience, organization, register and contextual clues.\n\n## Conhecimentos prévios\n- Recognize common English vocabulary in context\n- Identify who is speaking and what a short text is about\n\n## Conceitos fundamentais\n- Purpose and audience guide wording and organization\n- Register ranges from informal to formal and depends on relationship and setting\n- Links, images, emojis and layout can add meaning but must be interpreted in context\n\n## Explicação passo a passo\nBefore reading a message, identify its situation: who sent it, to whom, through which channel and for what purpose. A reminder, invitation and complaint can use similar words but require different responses.\n\nLook at organization and register. A subject line, greeting, short paragraphs and explicit request help a formal email. A group chat may be brief and informal, but clarity and respect still matter.\n\nDigital texts can combine words with links, icons, images and timing. Treat those signals as evidence, not proof: a forwarded message is not verified merely because it looks official, and an emoji can soften or change a tone depending on the relationship.\n\n## Aplicação\nCareful digital communication supports study groups, applications, collaborative projects and responsible evaluation of online information.\n\n## Erros frequentes\n- Choosing a register without considering the audience\n- Assuming a message is trustworthy because it has a logo or urgent wording\n- Writing an unclear request without a deadline or context\n\n## Resumo\nRead digital messages through purpose, audience, register and evidence; make requests clear and verify claims before sharing them.\n\n## Para praticar\nWhen answering, point to words or design choices that support your interpretation and avoid inferring facts that the message does not provide.\n\n## Reforços para dificuldades comuns\n### AUDIENCE REGISTER\n**Dificuldade:** Register does not fit the audience\n\nRegister is a choice, not a fixed ranking of vocabulary. Match formality to the relationship and task while keeping the message respectful and clear.\n\n**Exemplo:** “Send it now” may sound abrupt in a request to a new collaborator; “Could you send it by 3 p.m.?” makes the action and deadline clear.\n\n**Confira:** Would the wording feel appropriate if read by the intended recipient in this situation?\n\n### VERIFY DIGITAL CLAIM\n**Dificuldade:** Treating visual design as proof\n\nA logo, forwarded label or urgent tone does not verify the source. Check the sender and confirm consequential claims through an independent trusted channel.\n\n**Exemplo:** A message claiming that a school schedule changed should be checked against the school's official channel before it is forwarded.\n\n**Confira:** What independent evidence confirms the sender and the claim?",
        "tips": ["When answering, point to words or design choices that support your interpretation and avoid inferring facts that the message does not provide.", "Would the wording feel appropriate if read by the intended recipient in this situation?", "What independent evidence confirms the sender and the claim?"],
